    {
      "conversation": "topic-switch",
      "question": "Tell me about radar",
      "requestedIds": [
        "/encyclopedia/aircraft/mig-15/"
      ],
      "topic": [],
      "status": 200,
      "revision": "16ce32407976faa1f21d7ac3c205a6ce19be891c",
      "reply": {
        "answer": "Pulse-Doppler radar measures range by timing and finds angle and closure via the Doppler shift of each return, which it uses to reject clutter and track targets in velocity. A target flying across the line of sight falls in the Doppler notch. Pulse repetition frequency (PRF) is the rate a radar sends pulses and determines how far the radar can measure range unambiguously and how well it measures velocity. A higher PRF results in fewer blind speeds but more range ambiguity. Most modern pulse-Doppler radars use medium PRF for easy range and velocity tracking, and high PRF for excellent velocity resolution despite range ambiguity. Radars may also stagger or jitter the interval between pulses to reject range-ambiguous returns and combat repeater jammers. Radar correlation involves matching a radar contact to a controller's call by placing the cursor and scan volume at the called bearing, range, and altitude. The radar horizon is the distance where the earth's curve hides a low target, calculated using an effective earth radius 4/3 of the real one; for example, a radar at 1 mile altitude has a horizon of about 102 miles. A Radar Warning Receiver (RWR) is an electronic warfare support system that detects radar signals, identifies them against an emitter table, and displays them as symbols at a rough relative bearing with tones for new threats, tracks, and missile launches. An RWR provides a threat's type, rough bearing, and mode, but never a fix, and provides nothing for passive infrared missiles.",
        "ids": [
          "/encyclopedia/terminology/#pulse-doppler-radar",
          "/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-horizon",
          "/encyclopedia/terminology/#radar-warning-receiver",
